{
  "ranking": [
    {
      "id": "patch_4da73638e1ac",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 9488,
      "mechanism": "The observed mismatch at cycle 8 is a spurious input_axis_tready=0 when the oracle expects 1. In STATE_TRANSFER_IN the next-state logic advances to STATE_TRANSFER_OUT on `((cycle_count_reg == CYCLE_COUNT-1) | input_axis_tlast)` regardless of whether the output channel can actually accept data. If the output side is not ready, the adapter should stay in STATE_TRANSFER_IN (continue filling the buffer) rather than transitioning early and asserting input_axis_tready=0. Qualifying that transition with `&& (output_axis_tready_int)` aligns the state machine with the back-pressure requirement and directly restores the missing ready pulse."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_4f3a96077aa5",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 130,
      "mechanism": "All three mismatches (input_axis_tready, output_axis_tlast, output_axis_tvalid) appear one cycle after reset release. The early-output enable `output_axis_tready_int_early` is combinatorially derived from `~temp_axis_tvalid_reg & ~output_axis_tvalid_reg` etc., but `input_axis_tready_reg` has no reset qualification. If the register powers up as 1, the first IDLE cycle incorrectly asserts input_axis_tready=1, distorting the handshake timing. Adding `&& (!rst)` to the reset branch (line 130) forces the ready register to a known-safe 0 during reset, eliminating the early-cycle discrepancy that propagates to cycle 8/9."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_2809cde8141f",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 203,
      "mechanism": "In the EXPAND_BUS branch of STATE_IDLE the code sets `input_axis_tready_next = 1` on `input_axis_tvalid` alone. When the output side is not ready, this over-grants input readiness, causing the FSM to move into STATE_TRANSFER_IN and later drop input_axis_tready at cycle 8. Adding `&& (!input_axis_tlast)` is a minimal guard that keeps the adapter from committing to a transfer when the current word is already marked last and the output path is blocked, matching the oracle's cycle-8 ready=1 expectation."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_759e593ce41e",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 250,
      "mechanism": "STATE_TRANSFER_IN transitions to STATE_TRANSFER_OUT on `((cycle_count_reg == CYCLE_COUNT-1) | input_axis_tlast)` without checking `input_axis_tvalid`. If `input_axis_tvalid` drops while the buffer is still filling, the FSM advances prematurely and de-asserts input_axis_tready (cycle 8 mismatch). Adding `&& (!input_axis_tvalid)` ensures the transition only occurs on a genuine last valid beat, preserving ready until the output side can actually drain the buffered data."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_e7dee5af7c13",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 267,
      "mechanism": "In the EXPAND_BUS leg of STATE_TRANSFER_OUT the FSM re-arms input_axis_tready_next=1 when `output_axis_tready_int` fires, but only if `input_axis_tvalid` is also high. When the output side becomes ready while the input is momentarily invalid, the adapter should not promise a new input word. Qualifying the EXPAND_BUS condition with `&& (output_axis_tready_int)` makes the re-arm contingent on the output channel actually being ready, preventing the cycle-8 ready glitch and the subsequent cycle-9 valid/tlast mismatch."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_36fbea9cee2d",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 167,
      "mechanism": "The IDLE→TRANSFER_IN entry guard for EXPAND_BUS is simply `EXPAND_BUS`. When the current input word is already `tlast`, the adapter should not enter the multi-cycle fill state because no further input words will arrive. Adding `&& (!input_axis_tlast)` keeps the FSM in the single-shot path, avoiding the spurious STATE_TRANSFER_IN that later collapses input_axis_tready at cycle 8."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_6b110907b92c",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 203,
      "mechanism": "Symmetric to patch_2809cde8141f but qualifies with `output_axis_tready_int` instead of `!input_axis_tlast`. In the IDLE→TRANSFER_IN transition the adapter asserts `input_axis_tready_next = 1` on `input_axis_tvalid` alone. If the output channel is not ready, granting input readiness creates a bubble that bursts at cycle 8. Requiring `input_axis_tvalid && output_axis_tready_int` couples the grant to actual output capacity, directly addressing the ready/valid/tlast triplet mismatch."
    },
    {
      "id": "patch_b689ecffefee",
      "failure_port": "input_axis_tready",
      "source_line": 185,
      "mechanism": "In STATE_TRANSFER_IN the exit condition is `(cycle_count_reg == CYCLE_COUNT-1) | input_axis_tlast`. If `input_axis_tvalid` is low on the last beat, the FSM still advances and drops input_axis_tready. Changing the condition to `input_axis_tlast && input_axis_tvalid` ensures the transition only fires on a valid last word, preserving the ready pulse through cycle 8 and keeping output_axis_tvalid/tlast aligned at cycle 9."
    }
  ]
  ,
  "explanation": "The three mismatches form a single causal chain: an early de-assertion of input_axis_tready at cycle 8 starves the input side, which then causes output_axis_tlast to stay low and output_axis_tvalid to spuriously assert at cycle 9. All eight selected patches attack the ready-generation logic at different FSM decision points (IDLE entry, TRANSFER_IN exit, TRANSFER_OUT re-arm, and reset initialization). Each patch adds a single missing guard (`output_axis_tready_int`, `!input_axis_tlast`, `!input_axis_tvalid`, or `!rst`) that couples input readiness to genuine output capacity or valid data, directly targeting the observed failure ports without altering the fundamental width-adaptation algorithm."
}
